# Kaggle pack export -- build_kaggle_pack.ps1 lands pack\ at the repo root
# by default: ~40 MB of Linux ELF binaries, regenerable in minutes with that
# script, so it must never enter git history (nor the Docker build context).
pack/

# Staging directory used by build-native.yml: `--output type=local` writes the
# pack-exporter image's ROOT filesystem here, so the pack arrives nested as
# pack-export/pack/... The workflow then normalises it to pack/. Kept separate
# from `pack/` on purpose -- collapsing the export into ./pack instead would
# produce pack/pack/bin/..., which is exactly the bug this name avoids.
pack-export/

__pycache__/
*.pyc

# Gate-owned mypy cache. tools/lint_gate.py pins --cache-dir here so that
# running `mypy` by hand cannot change the gate's verdict -- see the reasoning
# on TOOL_MODULES in that file. Separate from .mypy_cache for exactly that
# reason: the two must not share state.
.mypy_cache_gate/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Build artifacts that are NOT covered by .gitattributes LFS rules
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# NOTE: *.exe is deliberately NOT here. .gitattributes routes it through LFS,
# and build-native.yml publishes the four Windows binaries as a CI artifact.
# That is a working arrangement; do not "fix" it by ignoring them.
#
# MSVC debug symbols: 4 files, 6.9 MB each, ~28 MB in total. Pure build output
# -- the .exe is identical without them, and `cmake --build` regenerates them in
# seconds. No LFS rule, so these sit in git as raw blobs.
*.pdb

# Compiler intermediate. CMake keeps these under .build/ (already ignored);
# this catches the strays that land at the root.
*.obj
*.ilk

# Release bundles produced by the local PowerShell scripts. CI rebuilds both
# from source as artifacts; keeping the archives means every commit that
# touched a binary added another 10 MB of compressed copy that can never be
# garbage collected.
kaggle-tdubber-pack.zip
windows-native-tools.zip
dist/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# projects/ -- the runtime data directory. Source code NEVER goes here.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Every project folder holds, by construction:
#
#   <name>.mp4           the dubbed output video
#   project.json         project metadata, written by the pipeline
#   dataset_safe/        a SNAPSHOT OF THIS REPO'S OWN SOURCE (see below)
#   kernel_safe/         the Kaggle kernel copy
#   pylibs/              a vendored Pillow, unpacked for offline use
#
# Nothing in it is authored by hand; everything is derived. It is input and
# output for app.py, which reads it at runtime:
#   app.py:24   PROJECTS_DIR
#   app.py:70   db.import_existing_projects(PROJECTS_DIR)
#
# WHY THE STALE SNAPSHOTS MATTER MOST
# MEASURED 2026-10-16: 717 tracked files under dataset_safe/ were byte-copies of
# first-party modules (multitasker.py, huggingface/hf_store.py, lip_sync.py,
# mazinger/*). Three reasons they must not be in git:
#   1. They DRIFT. The multitasker.py in the sampled snapshot no longer matched
#      the root file, so there were two "correct" versions and no way to tell
#      which one a reader should trust.
#   2. `ruff check .` and mypy both walk them, checking the same module once per
#      job folder. This is most of why the lint baseline exists at all.
#   3. Every fix to a real module had to be made N times over, or the copies
#      became a permanent source of "works on Kaggle, not locally".
#
# Untracked with `git rm --cached` -- the files stay on disk, because the
# pipeline reads them at runtime. Nothing here was deleted.
projects/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Stray scratch files that accumulated at the repo root
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# pwd.txt held a real credential. `=` is an accidental shell redirect artifact.
# dummy.mp4 is a 0-byte placeholder used as an upload argument in one manual
# test. None of these are source, data, or fixtures.
pwd.txt
taruntest.txt
test_chunk.txt
dummy.mp4
/=

# CMake build tree for the C++ normalizer.
#
# This directory was committed by accident -- 22 files including CMakeCache.txt,
# .ninja_deps, .ninja_log, a compiler-id binary and normalizer.exe itself. That
# is not merely untidy: CMakeCache.txt records ABSOLUTE paths of the machine that
# generated it, so the moment CI checked it out, cmake refused to configure --
#
#   CMake Error: The current CMakeCache.txt directory
#   'D:/a/T_Dubber/T_Dubber/cpp_accelerator/build/CMakeCache.txt' is different
#   than the directory 'C:/Program Files/Git/mingw64/lib/Git/cmake-build/...'
#   where 'CMakeCache.txt' was created.
#
# The recorded path was a Git-Bash-mangled path from the original author's
# machine, so it could never match any CI runner. A build tree is regenerated in
# seconds by the CI job that needs it, so it has no business in history.
cpp_accelerator/build/

# The scalar-only (-DNORMALIZER_ASM=OFF) twin tree, used to byte-compare the
# AVX2 kernel against the reference path: a generated tree for the same
# reasons, and never a source file.
cpp_accelerator/build_scalar/
cpp_accelerator/build_mix/

# Scratch tree written by update_kaggle_pack.ps1 (8 MB tar.gz + unpacked copy).
_pack_stage/

# Same reasoning for any other stray CMake tree.
CMakeCache.txt
CMakeFiles/
cmake_install.cmake
.ninja_deps
.ninja_log
compile_commands.json

# Out-of-tree build directory used by .github/workflows/build-native.yml. The
# Windows job builds normalizer.exe here instead of inside cpp_accelerator/, so
# a CMakeCache.txt can never be committed into the source tree by accident.
.build/

# Security: ignore Kaggle credentials
kaggle.json

# Security: ignore Hugging Face credentials.
# HuggingFace_PaperWork/ holds screenshots (fine to commit)
# but never a live token: hf_store.resolve_token() reads
# these names, so they must never reach git.
HuggingFace_PaperWork/hf.env
HuggingFace_PaperWork/.env
HuggingFace_PaperWork/token.txt
HuggingFace_PaperWork/tokens.txt

# Vendored NASM (tools/nasm/) -- downloaded by hand, ~1.5 MB of binaries that
# cpp_accelerator/CMakeLists.txt finds through its HINTS. Assembler, not
# source: regenerable from https://www.nasm.us/pub/nasm/releasebuilds/, so it
# has no business in git. The .asm files it assembles DO live in git.
tools/nasm/

# tgup session verdict cache, written by real bench/upload/fetch round
# trips (tgup_bridge.record_session_state). Runtime state, not source.
tgup.session.state.json

# Telegram session files -- THESE ARE LOGIN CREDENTIALS, not data.
#
# A Telethon/gotd .session file holds the account's auth key. Anyone who clones
# the repo can connect as that account: read the file, open a client, done. No
# OTP, no password. That is the same class of secret as api_hash, and it is
# worse, because api_hash alone cannot log in and an auth key can.
#
# MEASURED 2026-10-09: three of these were TRACKED and their commits were
# already PUSHED to origin (github.com/engrtarun/T_Dubber):
#   tgup.session, x.session, telegram_uploader_session.session
# in commits dee747b, bfa63a9, 31ece3f, f117a5d, 198962f, 4efa7b4.
# Untracked with `git rm --cached` (files stay on disk -- the uploader needs
# them). That stops the next commit from carrying a key; it does NOT un-publish
# the old ones. See WE_ARE_TEAM.MD 2026-10-09 21:52 for the rotation step that
# actually invalidates them.
*.session
*.session-journal
*.session.bak

# infra/_staging/ is what `python -m infra push` builds: a copy of the
# authorised tgup.session plus its dataset-metadata.json, waiting to be handed
# to the Kaggle CLI. The *.session rule above already covers the key itself;
# this covers the directory, so a future change to the staging layout cannot
# quietly put a credential in git. See infra/publish.py and SECURITY.md.
infra/_staging/

# Terraform working files. infra/ is a real module directory today but declares
# no resources, so these should never appear -- ignored anyway so that adding
# the first provider does not begin by committing state and secrets.
.terraform/
*.tfstate
*.tfstate.*
crash.log
override.tf
override.tf.json
*_override.tf
*_override.tf.json

# One-time login codes, passed to a script on stdin by whoever is logging in.
# These are single-use, but a committed one is a committed login attempt, and
# the habit that produces them is the habit that eventually commits the session.
otp*.txt
code*.txt

# The SQLite database is runtime state, not source: db.connect()
# creates and migrates it on first run (CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS +
# user_version pragma), so a fresh clone needs no committed DB -- and
# a binary blob that changes on every run only pollutes history.
# Removed from the index with `git rm --cached`; the file stays on
# disk, and every tool that reads it (app, dashboard_server, doctor,
# havaldar) goes through db.connect(), which recreates it.
t_dubber.db
t_dubber.db-shm
t_dubber.db-wal

target/
**/target/

# Local personal scratch & separate projects (protect secrets)
drive/
Dustbin 4 Code/
deepclonewebsite/
*.lock
telegram_uploader_session.lock
